.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd October 5, 2026
.Dt NVGPU 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nvgpu
.Nd NVIDIA Turing graphics driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To load the driver at run time:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kldload nvgpu
.Ed
To load the driver during system startup after filesystems are mounted,
add the following line to
.Xr rc.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nvgpu_load="YES"
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver provides graphics and compute command submission, GPU virtual
memory, and kernel mode setting for supported NVIDIA Turing GPUs.
It uses NVIDIA's GPU System Processor (GSP) firmware to initialize and
manage the hardware.
The driver implements the nouveau DRM userspace ABI so that Mesa's NVK
Vulkan driver can use it.
Mesa's Zink driver provides OpenGL through Vulkan.
The kernel driver does not itself implement either graphics API.
.Pp
The driver supplies primary and render nodes through
.Xr drm 4 .
Wayland compositors and X servers use the primary node for display
configuration and page flips.
Rendering and compute applications can use the render node without
becoming the DRM master.
Access to either node is subject to device permissions and, where
applicable, the desktop's seat management policy.
.Pp
Supported interfaces include GEM buffer objects, memory mapping, PRIME
buffer sharing, explicit VM_BIND and EXEC synchronization, binary and
timeline synchronization objects, and atomic mode setting.
Userspace must supply the dependencies between VM_BIND and EXEC
operations; the driver does not insert a process-wide implicit barrier
between them.
.Ss Hardware
The PCI device table contains TU102, TU104, and TU106 devices in the
GeForce RTX 20-series, TITAN RTX, and Quadro RTX families.
The GeForce RTX 2080 Ti (TU102) has been tested.
A PCI ID match for another board does not imply equivalent validation,
particularly for laptop display routing.
.Pp
Other GPU generations, including Ampere, Ada, and Blackwell, are not
supported by this version.
Video decoding and encoding engines are not exposed.
HDMI or DisplayPort audio also requires the appropriate audio driver,
normally
.Xr snd_hda 4 .
.Ss Firmware
The driver requires the file-backed firmware loading support provided by
.Xr firmware 9
and an installed NVIDIA firmware package.
It requests the following names:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nvidia/tu102/acr/bl.bin
nvidia/tu102/gsp/booter_load-570.144.bin
nvidia/tu102/gsp/booter_unload-570.144.bin
nvidia/tu102/gsp/bootloader-570.144.bin
nvidia/tu102/gsp/gsp-570.144.bin
.Ed
.Pp
Names are relative to the firmware search path, whose default includes
.Pa /usr/local/lib/firmware
and
.Pa /usr/lib/firmware .
TU104 and TU106 use the same
.Pa tu102
firmware directory.
Version 570.144 is selected by the driver; there is no runtime firmware
version selector.
Firmware files are distributed separately from the kernel module and
remain subject to NVIDIA's firmware license.
.Pp
Install all five files.
The unload booter is needed to release protected firmware memory during
detach so that a later attach can succeed.
Although an initial attach can proceed without it, a missing unload
booter is not a configuration suitable for unload and reload testing.
Do not substitute a different firmware version by renaming files.
.Ss Initialization
Loading
.Pa nvgpu.ko
also loads its DRM module dependency; it is not necessary to load
.Pa drm.ko
explicitly.
Firmware is obtained through
.Xr firmware 9 ,
not by explicitly loading a firmware kernel module.
.Pp
GPU initialization runs in a dedicated boot thread.
Successful return from
.Xr kldload 8
does not by itself mean that firmware initialization and DRM registration
have completed.
Check the kernel messages for initialization errors before starting a
display server.
The existence of a device node alone is not a readiness notification.
.Ss Memory and Synchronization
Each client has GPU virtual memory and command channels.
EXEC operations are scheduled when their input fences complete.
Hardware completion wakes the corresponding work, which signals the
completion fence used by synchronization objects.
Display commits wait for their input fences before programming scanout.
.Pp
Buffer mappings may outlive the file descriptor used to create them.
Closing a GEM handle or DRM descriptor does not release an outstanding
userspace mapping.
The driver does not implement eviction or live migration of bound
buffers; an allocation that cannot be satisfied fails rather than
evicting an existing buffer.
.Ss Unloading
To unload the GPU driver:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kldunload nvgpu
.Ed
.Pp
Active DRM clients, outstanding work, and surviving memory mappings can
cause unloading to fail with
.Er EBUSY .
Stop display servers and rendering applications and release their
mappings before retrying.
Unloading is intended for development and driver replacement.
It can remove the active display; restoration of the firmware console
is not guaranteed.
.Pp
The shared DRM module remains resident because its Linux compatibility
objects cannot currently be quiesced reliably for module unload.
Unload only
.Nm ;
do not attempt to force removal of its dependencies.
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
.Bl -tag -width "hw.nvgpu.debug"
.It Va hw.nvgpu.debug
Enable verbose driver messages when nonzero.
The default is zero.
This variable can be changed with
.Xr sysctl 8
after loading the module.
To capture early initialization messages, set
.Bd -literal -offset indent
hw.nvgpu.debug="1"
.Ed
in
.Xr loader.conf 5
before booting.
Console logging can perturb interrupt and scheduling timing;
disable it when measuring performance.
.El
.Pp
For lower-overhead tracing, the driver supplies
.Xr ktr 4
records under the
.Li nvgpu
name, with related shared-framework records under
.Li drm .
The module follows the kernel's KTR build configuration; it does not
require KTR in a kernel built without it.
NVGPU tracing is disabled by default, independently of
.Va hw.nvgpu.debug .
On a KTR-enabled kernel, use
.Va debug.ktr.nvgpu_enable
to select an event mask, then use
.Xr ktrdump 8
to inspect the records.
Set the mask back to zero after the diagnostic window.
Continuous tracing and frequent dumps can affect the workload being measured;
do not run a permanent collector for normal desktop use.
Do not assume that tracing is available merely because the module was
built with trace points.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/dev/dri/renderD*"
.It Pa /dev/dri/card*
Primary DRM nodes for display control and rendering.
.It Pa /dev/dri/renderD*
Render nodes for unprivileged rendering, subject to device permissions.
Minor numbers start at 128 and need not identify the first GPU.
.It Pa /usr/local/lib/firmware/nvidia/tu102
Usual location of the separately installed firmware files.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
When reporting a fault, include the PCI device ID, kernel and module
versions, Mesa version, relevant kernel messages, and a reproducer.
Distinguish successful device enumeration from successful rendering:
Vulkan extension enumeration alone does not exercise GPU execution.
.Pp
An initialization failure involving a firmware name should first be
checked against the installed files and the firmware search path.
An unload failure should first be checked for open DRM clients and
live mappings, including mappings whose original descriptor was closed.
